winewayland: show the last resolved cursor when the current handle cannot be resolved

Premiere Pro: click another window, click back on File, move down into the dropdown -- the
cursor vanishes the instant it crosses into the first item and stays gone while the menu is
open. Operator: "it doesn't disappear with time, it disappears with position".

Captured 2026-09-14 (premiere-20260914-160333.log, WINEDEBUG=+cursor,+waylanddrv, 11.10-88).
A freshly spawned helper process (its own Wayland client; the CEF window family) called
NtUserSetCursor(0x302b4) once, on a thread input it shares with Premiere's UI thread, so the
wineserver's current cursor for Premiere's windows became a handle that Premiere's process
cannot read -- win32u keeps cursor pixels per process:

    0644:trace:cursor:NtUserSetCursor 0x302b4
    13a0:trace:cursor:process_wine_setcursor hwnd 0x20128, window 0x20128, hcursor 0x302b4
    13a0:warn:cursor:get_icon_ptr icon handle 0x302b4 from other process
    13a0:err:waylanddrv:wayland_pointer_update_cursor_buffer Failed to get icon info for cursor=0x302b4
    ...
    13a4:trace:waylanddrv:pointer_handle_enter hwnd=0x302e6            <- the File dropdown
    13a0:trace:cursor:process_wine_setcursor hwnd 0x302e6, window 0x302e6, hcursor 0x302b4
    13a0:err:waylanddrv:wayland_pointer_update_cursor_buffer Failed to get icon info for cursor=0x302b4

wayland_pointer_update_cursor_buffer() answers a failed get_icon_info() with clear_cursor, and
wayland_set_cursor() then sends wl_pointer.set_cursor(NULL): the cursor is hidden. On the
main window nobody sees that, because the next WM_SETCURSOR replaces the handle with a live
one. A menu dropdown is a fresh surface: pointer_handle_enter() hides the cursor for it by
design and waits for the follow-up WM_WINE_SETCURSOR, the follow-up carried the same foreign
handle, and win32u sends no WM_SETCURSOR while a menu holds capture (process_mouse_message
returns before it when hwndCapture is set), so nothing ever replaced it. The popup 0x302e6 was
held from line 13428 to 14571 with the handle re-set and re-failing at every entry.

On Windows a cursor handle is usable from any process and the displayed image outlives the
handle (user32 tests: DestroyCursor on the active cursor leaves it current while GetIconInfo
fails); winex11 leaves the previous X cursor in place when create_cursor() fails. Hiding is
the wrong end of the failure.

Fix: remember the last cursor that resolved -- bitmap plus unscaled hotspot, or cursor-shape
enum -- at the outcome of a successful resolve, and when a non-NULL handle fails to resolve,
show that instead of nothing; with nothing retained, show the default arrow via the shape
protocol. A NULL handle (explicit hide, or a fresh surface awaiting its follow-up) and a fully
transparent cursor still hide: those are resolved outcomes, not failures. Covers every
unresolvable handle (foreign, destroyed), not only this one.

Not fixed by the existing cross-process cursor sync: that block keys on the WINDOW under the
pointer being foreign, and here the window is ours while the HANDLE is foreign; its data source
(a shape published by the owning process's driver in WAYLAND_SetCursor) is never written in this
path either, because the WM_WINE_SETCURSOR goes to the window's process, not the handle's.

Disproven on the way: a destroyed-handle use-after-free (single-process harness: the menu's own
WM_SETCURSOR sets a fresh arrow, cursor stays), a serial/xdg_popup mismatch (the popup's enter
serial is used and accepted; the same path is healthy in premiere-20260914-144822.log), pointer
clip/lock/relative mode (zero events in every session), and an owner-drawn/CEF dropdown (it is
a stock #32768 promoted to xdg_popup).

Kill switch NEUTRON_CURSOR_KEEP=0 restores the upstream hide for A/B without a rebuild;
NEUTRON_WL_DIAG=1 prints one neutron-cursorkeep line per substitution, at the outcome. Compiled
on both arches. Not a workaround for Adobe: Windows tolerates the foreign handle because cursors
are global there; Wine's per-process cursor pixels are the upstream gap.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_pointer.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_pointer.c
@@ -666,10 +666,82 @@
     return TRUE;
 }
 
-static void wayland_pointer_update_cursor_buffer(HCURSOR hcursor, double scale)
+/* NEUTRON: keep the last cursor that DID resolve when the current handle cannot be turned into pixels.
+ *
+ * The compositor shows whatever wl_pointer.set_cursor last named, per surface entry. The driver
+ * re-resolves the CURRENT Win32 cursor HANDLE into a bitmap at every entry and at every cursor-window
+ * change, and when get_icon_info() fails it falls through to set_cursor(NULL): the cursor is hidden.
+ * That is the wrong end of the failure. Windows keeps displaying the last cursor image whatever
+ * happens to the handle afterwards (user32 tests: DestroyCursor on the active cursor leaves it
+ * current while GetIconInfo fails), and winex11 simply leaves the previous X cursor in place.
+ *
+ * MEASURED 2026-09-14, Premiere Pro, log premiere-20260914-160333.log, WINEDEBUG=+cursor,+waylanddrv:
+ * a helper process (own Wayland client, CEF family) called NtUserSetCursor(0x302b4) on a thread input
+ * shared with Premiere's UI thread. win32u keeps cursor pixels per process, so Premiere's driver got
+ *     warn:cursor:get_icon_ptr icon handle 0x302b4 from other process
+ *     err:waylanddrv:wayland_pointer_update_cursor_buffer Failed to get icon info for cursor=0x302b4
+ * On the main window that is invisible: the next WM_SETCURSOR replaces the handle. Under a menu the
+ * popup is a FRESH surface -- pointer_handle_enter() issues set_cursor(NULL) by design and waits for
+ * the follow-up WM_WINE_SETCURSOR -- and the follow-up carried the same foreign handle, so nothing
+ * was ever attached and the cursor stayed hidden for the life of the menu. win32u sends no
+ * WM_SETCURSOR while a menu holds capture, so the app never got the chance to set a live one.
+ * Operator: "it doesn't disappear with time, it disappears with position".
+ *
+ * Fix: remember the last cursor that resolved (bitmap with its unscaled hotspot, or cursor-shape
+ * enum) at the OUTCOME of a successful resolve, and when a non-NULL handle fails to resolve, show
+ * that instead of nothing; with nothing retained, show the default arrow via the shape protocol.
+ * A NULL handle (an explicit hide, or a fresh surface awaiting its follow-up) still hides, and a
+ * fully transparent cursor still hides -- both are resolved outcomes, not failures. Covers every
+ * unresolvable handle (foreign, destroyed), not just this one.
+ *
+ * Kill switch NEUTRON_CURSOR_KEEP=0 restores the upstream hide; NEUTRON_WL_DIAG=1 reports each
+ * substitution at the outcome. All state below is guarded by process_wayland.pointer.mutex. */
+static struct
+{
+    HCURSOR hcursor;                             /* handle the retained cursor was built from (diag) */
+    struct wayland_shm_buffer *shm_buffer;       /* own ref; the last resolved bitmap, if any */
+    int hotspot_x, hotspot_y;                    /* in buffer pixels, unscaled */
+    enum wp_cursor_shape_device_v1_shape shape;  /* nonzero if the last resolved cursor was a shape */
+} neutron_cursor_last;
+
+static BOOL neutron_cursor_keep_enabled(void)
+{
+    static int v = -1;
+    if (v < 0) v = neutron_gate("NEUTRON_CURSOR_KEEP", TRUE);
+    return v;
+}
+
+static BOOL neutron_pointer_diag(void)
+{
+    static int v = -1;
+    if (v < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_WL_DIAG"); v = (e && e[0] == '1') ? 1 : 0; }
+    return v;
+}
+
+static void neutron_cursor_remember_buffer(HCURSOR hcursor, struct wayland_shm_buffer *buf, int hx, int hy)
+{
+    if (neutron_cursor_last.shm_buffer) wayland_shm_buffer_unref(neutron_cursor_last.shm_buffer);
+    wayland_shm_buffer_ref(buf);
+    neutron_cursor_last.shm_buffer = buf;
+    neutron_cursor_last.hotspot_x = hx;
+    neutron_cursor_last.hotspot_y = hy;
+    neutron_cursor_last.shape = 0;
+    neutron_cursor_last.hcursor = hcursor;
+}
+
+static void neutron_cursor_remember_shape(HCURSOR hcursor, enum wp_cursor_shape_device_v1_shape shape)
+{
+    neutron_cursor_last.shape = shape;
+    neutron_cursor_last.hcursor = hcursor;
+}
+
+/* NEUTRON: returns FALSE only when a non-NULL handle could not be resolved (get_icon_info or buffer
+ * creation failed). A NULL handle and a fully transparent cursor are resolved hides: TRUE. */
+static BOOL wayland_pointer_update_cursor_buffer(HCURSOR hcursor, double scale)
 {
     struct wayland_cursor *cursor = &process_wayland.pointer.cursor;
     ICONINFOEXW info = {0};
+    BOOL resolved = TRUE;
 
     if (!hcursor) goto clear_cursor;
 
@@ -683,6 +755,7 @@
     if (!get_icon_info(hcursor, &info))
     {
         ERR("Failed to get icon info for cursor=%p\n", hcursor);
+        resolved = FALSE;
         goto clear_cursor;
     }
 
@@ -707,6 +780,7 @@
     if (!cursor->shm_buffer)
     {
         ERR("Failed to create shm_buffer for cursor=%p\n", hcursor);
+        resolved = FALSE;
         goto clear_cursor;
     }
 
@@ -721,10 +795,13 @@
         cursor->hotspot_y = cursor->shm_buffer->height / 2;
     }
 
+    /* NEUTRON: this handle resolved -- record it at the outcome, before the hotspot is scaled. */
+    neutron_cursor_remember_buffer(hcursor, cursor->shm_buffer, cursor->hotspot_x, cursor->hotspot_y);
+
     cursor->hotspot_x = round(cursor->hotspot_x / scale);
     cursor->hotspot_y = round(cursor->hotspot_y / scale);
 
-    return;
+    return TRUE;
 
 clear_cursor:
     if (cursor->shm_buffer)
@@ -732,6 +809,7 @@
         wayland_shm_buffer_unref(cursor->shm_buffer);
         cursor->shm_buffer = NULL;
     }
+    return resolved;
 }
 
 static void wayland_pointer_clear_cursor_surface(void)
@@ -930,6 +1008,7 @@
 
     wp_cursor_shape_device_v1_set_shape(pointer->wp_cursor_shape_device_v1,
                                         pointer->enter_serial, shape);
+    neutron_cursor_remember_shape(hcursor, shape);
 
     return TRUE;
 }
@@ -977,6 +1056,57 @@
     }
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON: called with pointer->mutex held, for a non-NULL handle that did not resolve. Applies the
+ * last resolved cursor (or the default arrow shape) and returns TRUE; FALSE means the caller keeps
+ * the upstream behaviour (hide). See neutron_cursor_last above. */
+static BOOL neutron_cursor_keep_last(HWND hwnd, HCURSOR hcursor, double scale)
+{
+    struct wayland_pointer *pointer = &process_wayland.pointer;
+    struct wayland_cursor *cursor = &pointer->cursor;
+    const char *what;
+
+    if (!neutron_cursor_keep_enabled()) return FALSE;
+
+    if (neutron_cursor_last.shape || !neutron_cursor_last.shm_buffer)
+    {
+        enum wp_cursor_shape_device_v1_shape shape = neutron_cursor_last.shape ?
+            neutron_cursor_last.shape : WP_CURSOR_SHAPE_DEVICE_V1_SHAPE_DEFAULT;
+
+        if (!process_wayland.wp_cursor_shape_manager_v1) return FALSE;
+        if (!pointer->wp_cursor_shape_device_v1)
+            pointer->wp_cursor_shape_device_v1 =
+                wp_cursor_shape_manager_v1_get_pointer(
+                    process_wayland.wp_cursor_shape_manager_v1, pointer->wl_pointer);
+        if (!pointer->wp_cursor_shape_device_v1) return FALSE;
+        wayland_pointer_clear_cursor_surface();
+        wp_cursor_shape_device_v1_set_shape(pointer->wp_cursor_shape_device_v1,
+                                            pointer->enter_serial, shape);
+        what = neutron_cursor_last.shape ? "last shape" : "default shape (nothing retained)";
+    }
+    else
+    {
+        if (cursor->shm_buffer) wayland_shm_buffer_unref(cursor->shm_buffer);
+        wayland_shm_buffer_ref(neutron_cursor_last.shm_buffer);
+        cursor->shm_buffer = neutron_cursor_last.shm_buffer;
+        cursor->hotspot_x = round(neutron_cursor_last.hotspot_x / scale);
+        cursor->hotspot_y = round(neutron_cursor_last.hotspot_y / scale);
+        wayland_pointer_update_cursor_surface(scale);
+        if (!cursor->shm_buffer) return FALSE;
+        wl_pointer_set_cursor(pointer->wl_pointer,
+                              pointer->enter_serial,
+                              cursor->wl_surface,
+                              cursor->hotspot_x,
+                              cursor->hotspot_y);
+        wayland_pointer_clear_cursor_shape();
+        what = "last bitmap";
+    }
+
+    if (neutron_pointer_diag())
+        ERR("neutron-cursorkeep: hwnd=%p hcursor=%p unresolvable -> kept %s (built from hcursor=%p)\n",
+            hwnd, hcursor, what, neutron_cursor_last.hcursor);
+    return TRUE;
+}
+
 static void wayland_set_cursor(HWND hwnd, HCURSOR hcursor, BOOL use_hcursor)
 {
     struct wayland_pointer *pointer = &process_wayland.pointer;
@@ -1018,14 +1148,20 @@
         }
         else
         {
-            if (use_hcursor) wayland_pointer_update_cursor_buffer(hcursor, scale);
-            wayland_pointer_update_cursor_surface(scale);
-            wl_pointer_set_cursor(pointer->wl_pointer,
-                                  pointer->enter_serial,
-                                  pointer->cursor.wl_surface,
-                                  pointer->cursor.hotspot_x,
-                                  pointer->cursor.hotspot_y);
-            wayland_pointer_clear_cursor_shape();
+            BOOL resolved = TRUE;
+
+            if (use_hcursor) resolved = wayland_pointer_update_cursor_buffer(hcursor, scale);
+            /* NEUTRON: an unresolvable handle shows the last resolved cursor, not nothing. */
+            if (resolved || !hcursor || !neutron_cursor_keep_last(hwnd, hcursor, scale))
+            {
+                wayland_pointer_update_cursor_surface(scale);
+                wl_pointer_set_cursor(pointer->wl_pointer,
+                                      pointer->enter_serial,
+                                      pointer->cursor.wl_surface,
+                                      pointer->cursor.hotspot_x,
+                                      pointer->cursor.hotspot_y);
+                wayland_pointer_clear_cursor_shape();
+            }
         }
         wl_display_flush(process_wayland.wl_display);
         reapply_clip = TRUE;
